J713 DCP/DART error-path developer regressions
=============================================

Run manually from a kernel source checkout:

  python3 tools/testing/m4-dcp-errors/run.py

Requires Python 3 and a host GCC or Clang supporting UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer.
HOSTCC or --cc selects the compiler, including compiler wrappers. This is a
source-based developer test, not an installed kselftest. It creates temporary
host C units/executables and never builds a kernel, accesses hardware or runs CTS.
Nothing is generated in the source checkout.

Preserve generated sources, commands, source hashes and failure logs in a new
directory, and optionally run the real Kconfig dependency matrix using an
existing host scripts/kconfig/conf binary:

  python3 tools/testing/m4-dcp-errors/run.py \
    --source-root /path/to/kernel \
    --output-dir /path/to/new-results \
    --kconfig-conf /path/to/kernel-build/scripts/kconfig/conf

KCONFIG_CONF also selects that existing binary. The runner does not build it.
Without it the Kconfig matrix is explicitly reported as skipped; header-stub
linking and conditional module preprocessing still run. Run once with GCC and
once with Clang for both compiler controls.

The harness extracts the current production functions and register definitions.
Transport, MMIO, locks, pages and DRM context are synthetic host boundaries:

* Retained v2 IRQ routing: actual common handler and radio/display quarantine
  functions; owned and foreign SID faults; W1C exception ACK, barrier and readback.
  Neither retained profile may issue FLUSH_UNLOCK. The ordinary-domain control
  still requires encoded FLUSH_UNLOCK after ACK/barrier/readback. Both profiles
  cover v2.2 SID and v2.3 block exceptions, plus quiet and legacy routing.
  DMA-disable MMIO is a boundary mock here and covered by the radio SID tests.
  MMIO mocks implement W1C, not hardware timing.
* Shared-IRQ teardown: both display peers are quarantined before the action is
  freed, including the surviving peer. Partial-admission and ordinary IRQ
  removal are covered independently. Locks/IRQ synchronization are synthetic.
* Read-only admission: the actual probe predicate and pending-fault helper must
  notice v2 exception/block state and masked fault delivery without writes.
* Quarantined SID0 unmap: all pages across a child-table boundary must be pinned
  before full logical retirement. Existing hardware PTEs remain; a new map is
  refused by the sticky failure state. Failed pin, normal unmap and failed-flush
  PTE restoration controls remain distinct.
* Asynchronous callback failures: dispatch, finish and ACK errors must log the
  method/tag, lengths, stage and errno. Failed dispatch cannot send an ACK;
  successful callbacks produce no error diagnostic.
* Module-disabled public APIs must compile/link as EOPNOTSUPP stubs. The actual
  retained implementation fragments are conditionally preprocessed to ensure
  ordinary modular DART contains no private ARM64 DMA calls; the native graphics
  configuration fragment must explicitly opt in to the built-in retained feature.
* Optional Kconfig: unchanged production producer blocks plus synthetic unrelated
  dependencies cover ordinary modular DART, retained built-in, modular DRM with
  built-in DART, unsupported page size, and disabled retained feature. This is
  dependency reachability, not full kernel configuration or module linking.

Six built-in mutation controls must compile and fail at runtime: missing radio
or display quarantine, an unlocked retained display, an ordinary domain left
locked, loss of the surviving peer's quarantine, and masked fault admission.
Compile errors do not count as detected mutations.

An optional old source snapshot can act as an additional negative control:

  python3 tools/testing/m4-dcp-errors/run.py \
    --negative-source-root /path/to/old-kernel-source \
    --output-dir /path/to/new-results

Every production-function control must fail against that snapshot; it should
contain the old borrowed-v2 routing, zero-return quarantined unmap and silent
callback behavior. No historical source or captured firmware data is bundled.

These tests do not prove DMA cache coherency, firmware layout, real shared-IRQ
synchronization, interrupt delivery, resource lifetime during device removal,
full kernel Kconfig, ARM64 module linking, or successful display operation.
Full kernel/module builds and a fresh reboot with native KMS checks remain
separate qualification. A working fallback does not establish native firmware
clock, bandwidth or thermal-policy parity.
